00:04:56.540 --> 00:05:01.825
would go through a narrow area approximately about 45 feet. It's right,

00:05:02.545 --> 00:05:05.985
on the left side of the end of the street on the cul de sac,

00:05:07.185 --> 00:05:10.705
right where the cul de sac kind of starts, the round area of the cul de sac.

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:14.680
The

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:17.880
>> proposal involves us,

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:20.440
sort of following the grade of

00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:28.200
the area right off of Pine Ridge Road, and then as you know, if you live in the area,

00:05:28.815 --> 00:05:30.175
particularly

00:05:30.175 --> 00:05:35.775
on that side of Pine Ridge, it dips down, there's a corridor where these, wetlands areas,

00:05:36.735 --> 00:05:39.055
go through. One branch comes in,

00:05:40.015 --> 00:05:41.055
behind all of the,

00:05:42.070 --> 00:05:48.630
the watershed is behind a lot of the houses on Pine Ridge. The other actually comes down. It's where the,

00:05:49.670 --> 00:05:50.390
the

00:05:50.710 --> 00:05:53.190
water course coming down from Old Farms Road

00:05:53.350 --> 00:05:53.990
flows through.

00:05:55.265 --> 00:06:01.505
Once we get down, once you get down into the low lying area, the plan involves raising the

00:06:02.385 --> 00:06:04.545
crossing up approximately

00:06:04.545 --> 00:06:05.905
five or six feet,

00:06:06.545 --> 00:06:06.945
and

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:11.060
we would have interlocking

00:06:11.060 --> 00:06:14.100
concrete blocks to kind of create the outer

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:17.620
walls and then fill the interior portion of it,

00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:20.820
and put a gravel

00:06:21.940 --> 00:06:26.115
driveway on top of it. The two streams that go underneath this crossing,

00:06:27.075 --> 00:06:28.355
would flow through

00:06:30.595 --> 00:06:31.395
on the

00:06:33.075 --> 00:06:34.355
western side,

00:06:34.435 --> 00:06:38.150
eastern side, forgive me, a three foot culvert

00:06:38.150 --> 00:06:38.790
pipe

00:06:39.030 --> 00:06:40.550
would be embedded,

00:06:41.030 --> 00:06:42.310
25%,

00:06:42.790 --> 00:06:47.190
of the, of the area of a cross section would be embedded into the, into the ground,

00:06:48.735 --> 00:06:53.135
and the eastern stream would flow through a larger,

00:06:54.255 --> 00:06:59.215
eight foot wide by four foot tall concrete box culvert.

00:06:59.615 --> 00:07:01.855
That too would be embedded

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:04.160
with about

00:07:04.640 --> 00:07:07.200
25% of its area would be embedded under

00:07:08.480 --> 00:07:12.720
the ground. That last part there, box culvert is one of the

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:19.600
more recent changes to the plan. We originally were going to have let me hold on a normal pipe to with

00:07:22.185 --> 00:07:26.105
comply with potentially another jurisdiction from the Army Corps

00:07:26.665 --> 00:07:27.545
of Engineers,

00:07:29.120 --> 00:07:32.720
which were in the process, still in the process of working with a

00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:38.240
bigger opening was required to comply with one

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:40.240
of their standards.

00:07:44.355 --> 00:07:45.075
I

00:07:45.235 --> 00:07:46.435
think that kind of

00:07:46.755 --> 00:07:48.115
generally describes,

00:07:48.755 --> 00:07:52.035
what's happening. Think, so, you know, the driveway would go down,

00:07:52.755 --> 00:07:55.315
hit to that bottom area and then kind of go back up,

00:07:55.875 --> 00:07:58.755
and it would go in, allow access to

00:08:01.210 --> 00:08:05.690
one of the, one of two homes that will be built on the rear lot that's,

00:08:05.930 --> 00:08:06.570
that's,

00:08:09.050 --> 00:08:15.050
that this driveway kind of, that this opening in from Pine Ridge gives, access to that property.

00:08:15.965 --> 00:08:22.125
I think, you know, I don't know that I have anything else, at least in my head to add to this, so maybe I can ask,

00:08:22.765 --> 00:08:27.325
either George Correa or George Logan if you guys have anything else to add. George Logan,

00:08:28.140 --> 00:08:29.260
the

00:08:29.260 --> 00:08:30.380
quick introduction,

00:08:30.540 --> 00:08:31.420
George

00:08:31.420 --> 00:08:32.300
has

00:08:32.300 --> 00:08:35.180
represented us in a couple of ways. He's

00:08:36.140 --> 00:08:38.780
evaluated the ecological impact.

00:08:38.940 --> 00:08:39.980
He's also a

00:08:42.620 --> 00:08:43.020
scientist.

00:08:43.505 --> 00:08:46.865
He could probably speak to a range of factors

00:08:46.865 --> 00:08:47.425
here

00:08:48.545 --> 00:08:50.705
about the impact of the project,

00:08:51.345 --> 00:08:53.345
and George Correa certainly knows the

00:08:53.665 --> 00:08:55.745
details, the specific details of

00:08:56.220 --> 00:08:57.500
dimensions and

00:08:57.580 --> 00:09:03.180
the crossing and volumes and stuff like that better than I do. So I'll turn it over if you guys have anything else to add.

00:09:03.900 --> 00:09:04.780
Thank you for the time.

00:09:07.755 --> 00:09:09.915
>> Sure, this is George Logan,

00:09:09.995 --> 00:09:14.075
Rheem Ecological Services, soil scientist, wetland scientist, ecologist.

00:09:14.955 --> 00:09:16.555
There's been a couple reports

00:09:17.115 --> 00:09:18.235
that I've submitted

00:09:18.475 --> 00:09:19.275
into the record.

00:09:20.100 --> 00:09:21.700
I think the last one

00:09:22.420 --> 00:09:24.420
was about a month ago or so,

00:09:25.300 --> 00:09:26.100
I believe.

00:09:27.300 --> 00:09:29.860
Trying to find a date here. Yes, actually

00:09:30.500 --> 00:09:31.860
February 22.

00:09:32.955 --> 00:09:40.075
It's been a while. So at that time, one of the things we had been discussing prior to that was whether we would have stayed with

00:09:40.555 --> 00:09:42.235
the 36 inch pipe

00:09:42.555 --> 00:09:43.915
for both streams.

00:09:44.155 --> 00:09:45.675
And as you might recall,

00:09:45.915 --> 00:09:57.390
I've gone out and evaluated these streams. Yes, it was winter. Yes, there was snow on the ground, but you always can see with the aquatic organisms that are utilizing the stream. I was most more interested in the

00:09:57.710 --> 00:10:01.950
Eastern Stream because our hydraulic engineer had told us that

00:10:02.575 --> 00:10:10.575
the Western Watershed, which is a larger one, has about 40 acres, and it it has wetlands also associated. It's behind all those houses

00:10:11.135 --> 00:10:12.575
that are Pine Ridge.

00:10:12.895 --> 00:10:15.775
And the Eastern Watershed was only 23 acres.

00:10:16.270 --> 00:10:17.790
And as you will recall,

00:10:18.670 --> 00:10:21.550
whereas the Western Stream is almost,

00:10:22.910 --> 00:10:24.430
I wouldn't say it's level,

00:10:24.910 --> 00:10:29.790
it comes up but not at a high grade past the area of the crossing.

00:10:30.750 --> 00:10:33.215
And there are wetlands behind that to the north.

00:10:33.775 --> 00:10:34.575
The

00:10:34.735 --> 00:10:35.935
Eastern Stream,

00:10:36.095 --> 00:10:37.695
or the 23 acres,

00:10:39.295 --> 00:10:43.855
has a pretty steep section for about 400 or so feet before it flattens out at the top.

00:10:44.570 --> 00:10:48.490
And that's associated with the Red Maple Swamp at that point.

00:10:49.530 --> 00:10:51.370
So based on my evaluation

00:10:51.370 --> 00:10:51.930
of

00:10:52.490 --> 00:10:57.370
the aquatic life that could use that, I'll also have you remember we had discussions

00:10:58.355 --> 00:11:03.795
with Hank Gruner and the sensitivity of the general area, because of the Hop River nearby

00:11:04.275 --> 00:11:08.595
and, the potential that wood turtles could come up to this area

00:11:08.915 --> 00:11:10.035
for foraging,

00:11:10.435 --> 00:11:11.795
following the string courses.

00:11:12.420 --> 00:11:15.140
And as you recall, just below this crossing,

00:11:15.940 --> 00:11:19.140
the wetland is pretty flat and it's somewhat open.

00:11:19.940 --> 00:11:20.580
So

00:11:20.980 --> 00:11:21.620
during

00:11:21.860 --> 00:11:27.025
well, from now on and and during growing season, it will be replete with all kinds of

00:11:28.225 --> 00:11:30.705
herbaceous species that could potentially

00:11:32.625 --> 00:11:34.865
have provide for cover for

00:11:35.825 --> 00:11:39.345
the turtles as they come up the corridor, if they would, even though

00:11:40.230 --> 00:11:42.550
to the actual river itself,

00:11:43.190 --> 00:11:49.350
there's a section of about maybe a hundred, hundred and fifty feet, which is also a pretty steep gradient and sort of rocky and

00:11:51.030 --> 00:11:57.505
probably not the best place for turtles to pass, but it is possible to pass. So based on all our discussions that we had

00:11:58.305 --> 00:12:02.305
and the fact that we have to comply with certain things

00:12:03.025 --> 00:12:04.785
that the Army Corps of Engineers,

00:12:05.425 --> 00:12:09.020
voice on us, even though typically they do it only for,

00:12:09.260 --> 00:12:11.180
you know, higher order streams,

00:12:11.180 --> 00:12:12.220
higher order

00:12:12.540 --> 00:12:13.420
intermittent

00:12:13.420 --> 00:12:15.340
streams and also perennial streams,

00:12:16.060 --> 00:12:19.900
we decided we we we better go with the box

00:12:20.220 --> 00:12:20.940
culvert

00:12:21.795 --> 00:12:24.195
to comply with the openness ratio,

00:12:24.835 --> 00:12:29.315
even though we don't have to comply with part of it because it is an intermittent stream,

00:12:29.555 --> 00:12:30.195
such as

00:12:30.595 --> 00:12:33.875
being 1.2 times the flow path

00:12:34.500 --> 00:12:37.300
of stream. That's usually for a perineal stream.

00:12:37.860 --> 00:12:40.580
So we decided to put it on the western and

00:12:41.380 --> 00:12:42.420
maintain

00:12:43.060 --> 00:12:44.020
the

00:12:44.340 --> 00:12:45.700
36 inches culvert

00:12:46.215 --> 00:12:47.975
somewhat buried for

00:12:48.615 --> 00:12:51.815
the Eastern stream, not the West. The Western has the box culvert.

00:12:52.455 --> 00:12:59.575
Another thing that we we kinda threw in the mix, one of the questions that was asked of us was how about mitigation? We explained that typically

00:13:00.620 --> 00:13:04.700
in kind mitigation is not a good idea when a site is

00:13:05.340 --> 00:13:08.060
relatively undisturbed unless there was an

00:13:09.420 --> 00:13:11.340
area that we could restore, enhance.

00:13:13.075 --> 00:13:13.795
So

00:13:13.955 --> 00:13:17.635
we decided that as far as mitigation is concerned, that we would

00:13:18.275 --> 00:13:19.795
do one thing

00:13:20.595 --> 00:13:24.115
up, as recall, the 23 acre or so

00:13:24.515 --> 00:13:30.440
eastern intermittent stream goes up the hill where it flattens about 400 feet from the crossing.

00:13:31.000 --> 00:13:33.400
It's unfortunately replete with

00:13:34.120 --> 00:13:38.120
Japanese barberry, which is an invasive species, an invasive shrub.

00:13:38.920 --> 00:13:39.800
So

00:13:39.800 --> 00:13:41.160
what we proposed

00:13:41.160 --> 00:13:42.360
was for

00:13:42.975 --> 00:13:44.975
where the stream comes up and

00:13:45.615 --> 00:13:50.015
the wetland corridor starts widening out and flattens

00:13:50.575 --> 00:13:51.935
is to remove

00:13:52.495 --> 00:13:53.775
Japanese

00:13:53.775 --> 00:13:54.575
barberry

00:13:55.160 --> 00:14:07.240
within 15 feet of the intermittent stream and for a length of at least 150 feet from where it crests up the hill and then follows up. I think by the time we get to 150 feet or so, it starts veering

00:14:08.920 --> 00:14:09.640
off-site.

00:14:10.200 --> 00:14:11.240
And

00:14:11.055 --> 00:14:14.895
the proposal will be simple, and and thankfully,

00:14:14.895 --> 00:14:15.535
Japanese

00:14:15.775 --> 00:14:18.415
barberry is one of the easiest species to get rid of.

00:14:18.895 --> 00:14:22.175
You can basically yank them. You don't have to use any

00:14:23.055 --> 00:14:26.095
refractory organics, any herbicides to

00:14:26.095 --> 00:14:26.815
to remove them.

00:14:27.800 --> 00:14:30.040
I've done it in my backyard out here,

00:14:30.600 --> 00:14:34.600
and it took me really one season to get rid of. But we're probably

00:14:34.840 --> 00:14:39.800
saying that it probably is gonna be a first season where the bulk of it is taken out mechanically.

00:14:39.880 --> 00:14:46.035
Usually the procedure for that is to wait for the soil to be moist after a rainstorm.

00:14:46.435 --> 00:14:49.075
And then you could use a a weed wench

00:14:49.395 --> 00:14:50.435
to basically

00:14:50.675 --> 00:14:55.155
take the whole thing out if possible. If it's too big, you cut it at the base.

00:14:55.555 --> 00:14:57.075
Remove all that

00:14:57.430 --> 00:14:58.230
material

00:14:58.950 --> 00:14:59.830
properly.

00:15:00.710 --> 00:15:04.230
And then the second year, you just remove seedlings.

00:15:04.230 --> 00:15:07.990
That's the only thing that probably will remain that would come up again.

00:15:08.310 --> 00:15:10.790
And after that time, we recommended

00:15:11.755 --> 00:15:13.595
that we could interplant

00:15:13.915 --> 00:15:18.155
in appropriate spaces to diversify the understory, particular

00:15:20.075 --> 00:15:27.355
by a minimum of 50 shrubs that could be planted. And I think the species that we selected are some ones that are out there already.

00:15:28.850 --> 00:15:30.610
For instance, spicebush,

00:15:30.690 --> 00:15:32.610
common winterberry,

00:15:33.250 --> 00:15:34.290
nannyberry,

00:15:34.290 --> 00:15:39.490
I think we had gray dogwood in there too, and a minimum of 50 species to be planted.

00:15:40.925 --> 00:15:41.565
So

00:15:42.205 --> 00:15:43.005
I think

00:15:43.645 --> 00:15:46.445
what we've done in the past few months here,

00:15:46.605 --> 00:15:50.525
after all the discussions that we had, where it was a significant improvement,

00:15:51.165 --> 00:15:53.565
especially with the box culvert

00:15:53.805 --> 00:15:56.525
at the Western Stream with bigger watershed.

00:15:57.290 --> 00:16:00.890
I know that at the last time, my my colleague, Hank Gruner,

00:16:01.610 --> 00:16:02.330
had

00:16:04.250 --> 00:16:06.410
a question as to whether there will be,

00:16:07.610 --> 00:16:09.130
a hydraulic issue,

00:16:10.490 --> 00:16:12.090
meaning that we were

00:16:12.385 --> 00:16:15.825
the box cover would potentially concentrate and erode the stream.

00:16:16.545 --> 00:16:18.145
Again, I mentioned

00:16:18.145 --> 00:16:20.385
at that time that I didn't feel qualified,

00:16:21.345 --> 00:16:22.385
specifically,

